            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from Chicago to Madrid?</h3>
            <p>January and February offer the lowest fares, typically $500-$850 round-trip in economy. For summer travel, book 45-70 days ahead and target April or October. Tuesday and Wednesday departures consistently undercut weekend prices by a meaningful margin.</p>


            <h3>Q2: Are there direct flights from Chicago to Madrid?</h3>
            <p>Iberia operates seasonal nonstop service from Chicago O'Hare to Madrid-Barajas, typically from late spring through early autumn. American codeshares on this flight. Flight duration is approximately 8 to 8.5 hours. During winter months, connections are required through European hubs.</p>


            <h3>Q3: Do I need a visa to visit Madrid from the US?</h3>
            <p>United States citizens do not require a visa for short tourist stays in Spain. The Schengen Agreement permits visits up to 90 days within any rolling 180-day period. A valid passport with at least three months' validity beyond your planned departure is required.</p>


            <h3>Q4: How much baggage can I bring on a Chicago to Madrid flight?</h3>
            <p>Most full-service carriers include one checked bag up to 50 pounds and one carry-on bag plus a personal item on transatlantic itineraries. Iberia includes baggage on most international fares. US legacy carriers may charge $35 for the first checked bag. Always verify your specific airline's policy.</p>


            <h3>Q5: What is the best way to get from Madrid-Barajas Airport to the city center?</h3>
            <p>The Metro Line 8 reaches Nuevos Ministerios in 30-40 minutes for approximately €5. The Cercanías train connects Terminal 4 to Atocha in 25-30 minutes for €2.60. Official taxis charge approximately a €30-€35 fixed rate, including a supplement. Express bus 203 serves Atocha station.</p>


            <h3>Q6: Can I use US dollars in Madrid, or do I need euros?</h3>
            <p>Spain uses the euro exclusively, and US dollars are not accepted. ATMs are abundant throughout Madrid, and credit cards work at most establishments. Contactless payment is widely preferred. Carry some cash for smaller purchases at tapas bars and markets that may prefer cash.</p>


            <h3>Q7: Is Madrid safe for American tourists?</h3>
            <p>Madrid is among Europe's safest major cities for visitors. Standard precautions apply regarding pickpockets in crowded tourist areas like Puerta del Sol, Gran Vía, and on the Metro. Violent crime against tourists is extremely rare. The city maintains a strong police presence and efficient emergency services.</p>


            <h3>Q8: What is the best area to stay in Madrid for first-time visitors?</h3>
            <p>Puerta del Sol and Gran Vía offer a central location and excellent transport links. Malasaña provides trendy nightlife and boutique shopping. La Latina is ideal for tapas and a traditional atmosphere. Salamanca delivers upscale elegance and designer shopping. Choose based on your priorities and budget.</p>


            <h3>Q9: How early should I arrive at O'Hare for my Madrid flight?</h3>
            <p>Arrive three hours before scheduled departure for international flights from O'Hare Terminal 5. During peak summer travel or holiday weekends, consider arriving 3.5 hours early. TSA PreCheck and CLEAR members can significantly reduce security wait times at O'Hare.</p>


            <h3>Q10: What happens if my flight to Madrid is delayed or cancelled?</h3>Airlines <p>must provide meals and accommodation for qualifying overnight delays depending on cause and duration. For cancellations, EU regulations mandate rebooking or refunds when flying EU-based carriers like Iberia. Most major airlines extend comparable protections voluntarily. Contact your airline immediately and maintain travel insurance for additional coverage.</p>






